Object.assign((window.COI_EXPLAIN = window.COI_EXPLAIN || {}), {
  "324": { "s": "An independent Election Commission runs all national and state elections.", "e": "This article gives the Election Commission of India full charge of preparing voter lists and running elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President. The Commission is headed by the Chief Election Commissioner, with as many other Election Commissioners as the President decides; since 1993 it has had three members. To protect its independence, the Chief Election Commissioner can be removed only in the same way as a Supreme Court judge (by Parliament through a special process), and his service conditions cannot be changed to his disadvantage after appointment. Other Election Commissioners can be removed only on the Chief Election Commissioner's recommendation. The Supreme Court has read the Commission's powers broadly, allowing it to act where the law is silent (Mohinder Singh Gill, 1978), which is the basis for tools like the Model Code of Conduct. In Anoop Baranwal (2023) the Court said appointments should be made on the advice of a committee (the Prime Minister, the Leader of the Opposition and the Chief Justice of India) until Parliament made a law; Parliament then passed a 2023 Act replacing the Chief Justice with a Union Cabinet Minister on the committee. Challenges to that Act are still pending in the Supreme Court. Governors and the President must provide the staff the Commission needs to conduct elections." },
  "325": { "s": "One common voter list per constituency; no exclusion by religion, race, caste or sex.", "e": "Every territorial constituency has a single general electoral roll for all voters. No one can be kept off that roll, or demand a separate roll, only because of religion, race, caste or sex. This deliberately ended the colonial system of separate electorates, where different communities voted only for their own candidates." },
  "326": { "s": "Every adult citizen aged 18 or above has the right to vote.", "e": "Elections to the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies are held on the basis of universal adult suffrage: every Indian citizen of the qualifying age can register as a voter, regardless of wealth, education or gender. The voting age was lowered from 21 to 18 by the 61st Amendment (1988). A person can be disqualified only on grounds set by the Constitution or by law, namely non-residence, unsoundness of mind, crime, or corrupt or illegal electoral practices. The details of registration are filled in by laws such as the Representation of the People Act, 1950. Adopting adult franchise from the very first election in 1951-52 was one of the boldest choices of the Constitution's framers." },
  "327": { "s": "Parliament can make laws on all aspects of elections.", "e": "Parliament may make laws on every matter connected with elections to Parliament and State Legislatures, such as voter lists, delimitation of constituencies and the conduct of polls. The main laws made under this power are the Representation of the People Acts of 1950 and 1951 and the Delimitation Acts. Such laws must still respect the rest of the Constitution." },
  "328": { "s": "States can make election laws for their legislatures where Parliament has not.", "e": "A State Legislature may make laws about elections to its own House or Houses, including voter rolls, but only on matters Parliament has not already covered. In practice, Parliament's laws cover most of the field, so this power is used for gaps only." },
  "329": { "s": "Courts cannot stop elections midway; disputes go by election petition.", "e": "Laws on delimitation of constituencies and allotment of seats cannot be challenged in any court. Also, an election to Parliament or a State Legislature can be questioned only through an election petition filed after the result, before the authority and in the manner fixed by law (the High Court, under the Representation of the People Act, 1951). The Supreme Court held in N.P. Ponnuswami (1952) that courts should not interfere while the election process is under way, so that elections are not delayed. Courts have, however, allowed limited review where the Election Commission's action is plainly outside the law." },
  "329A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly shielded the PM's and Speaker's elections from ordinary courts.", "e": "Inserted during the Emergency by the 39th Amendment (1975) to protect the Prime Minister's election from challenge; part of it was struck down by the Supreme Court in Indira Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975). It was removed by the 44th Amendment (1978)." },
  "330": { "s": "Lok Sabha seats are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes.", "e": "Seats in the Lok Sabha are reserved for Scheduled Castes, for Scheduled Tribes, and separately for Scheduled Tribes of Assam's autonomous districts. In each State or Union territory, the number of reserved seats is in proportion to the share of SCs or STs in its population. Reserved seats are not separate electorates: all voters of the constituency vote, but only SC or ST candidates may contest. The population figures used for this have been frozen by amendments (currently the 2001 census) until the figures of the first census taken after 2026 are published (Census 2027 is under way). Under Article 334, this reservation lasts until 2030." },
  "330A": { "s": "One-third of directly elected Lok Sabha seats to be reserved for women.", "e": "Added by the 106th Amendment (2023), often called the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam, this article reserves for women, as nearly as possible, one-third of the seats filled by direct election to the Lok Sabha. Within the seats already reserved for SCs and STs, one-third are also reserved for women of those communities, and these count towards the overall one-third. The reservation does not cover the Rajya Sabha. Importantly, it is not yet in operation: the Amendment was brought into force on 16 April 2026, but under Article 334A the reservation takes effect only after a delimitation exercise based on the first census held after that commencement. Once in force it lasts for fifteen years, which Parliament may extend, and the reserved seats rotate after each later delimitation." },
  "331": { "s": "President could nominate up to two Anglo-Indians to the Lok Sabha (now lapsed).", "e": "This allowed the President to nominate up to two members of the Anglo-Indian community to the Lok Sabha if the community was not adequately represented. Under Article 334, as amended by the 104th Amendment (2019), this provision stopped operating in January 2020, seventy years after the Constitution began. The text remains, but no such nominations are now made." },
  "332": { "s": "State Assembly seats are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes.", "e": "Every State Legislative Assembly must reserve seats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, in proportion to their share of the State's population. Assam has special rules: seats are reserved for its autonomous districts, and only members of the relevant tribes may contest them. As with the Lok Sabha, all voters of the constituency vote in a reserved seat. This reservation also runs until 2030 under Article 334." },
  "332A": { "s": "One-third of directly elected State Assembly seats to be reserved for women.", "e": "Added by the 106th Amendment (2023), this article requires every State Legislative Assembly to reserve, as nearly as possible, one-third of its directly elected seats for women. One-third of the seats already reserved for SCs and STs go to women from those groups, and these are part of the overall one-third. A matching provision for the Delhi Assembly was added to Article 239AA. The reservation does not apply to Legislative Councils. Like Article 330A, it will start only after a fresh delimitation based on the first census after the Amendment's commencement, as provided in Article 334A, and lasts fifteen years unless Parliament extends it." },
  "333": { "s": "Governor could nominate one Anglo-Indian to a State Assembly (now lapsed).", "e": "This allowed a Governor to nominate one member of the Anglo-Indian community to the State Assembly if the community needed and lacked representation. Under Article 334, as amended by the 104th Amendment (2019), it ceased to operate in January 2020." },
  "334": { "s": "Sets end dates: SC/ST seat reservation lasts 80 years; Anglo-Indian nomination ended at 70.", "e": "The framers intended political reservations to be temporary, so this article puts a time limit on them. Originally the limit was ten years, but it has been extended by amendment every ten years since. Under the 104th Amendment (2019), reservation of seats for SCs and STs in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies lasts eighty years from 26 January 1950, that is, until January 2030. Nomination of Anglo-Indians was allowed to end at seventy years, in January 2020. A sitting House is not affected by the expiry until it is dissolved. Note that this article covers only legislative seats; reservation in jobs and education rests on other provisions and has no such fixed end date." },
  "334A": { "s": "Women's reservation starts after a post-census delimitation and lasts fifteen years.", "e": "Inserted by the 106th Amendment (2023), this article sets when the reservation of seats for women in the Lok Sabha, State Assemblies and the Delhi Assembly begins and ends. It comes into effect only after delimitation of constituencies is carried out using the figures of the first census taken after the Amendment's commencement (it was brought into force on 16 April 2026); so the reservation is not immediate. As of November 2026, Census 2027 is still under way and no such delimitation has taken place. It lasts for fifteen years from that commencement, but Parliament can by law continue it beyond that. Which seats are reserved for women will rotate after each subsequent delimitation, as Parliament decides by law. A House already sitting is not affected until it is dissolved." },
  "335": { "s": "SC/ST claims to government jobs must be considered, consistent with efficiency.", "e": "When appointments are made to posts under the Union or a State, the claims of Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes must be taken into account, while keeping administrative efficiency in mind. A proviso added by the 82nd Amendment (2000) makes clear that the government may relax qualifying marks or lower evaluation standards for SC/ST candidates, including for reservation in promotions. Courts have treated efficiency as a relevant factor when testing reservation schemes in promotion." },
  "336": { "s": "Temporary job quotas for Anglo-Indians in certain services, now expired.", "e": "For the first ten years of the Constitution, Anglo-Indians kept a share of posts in the railway, customs, postal and telegraph services, reduced by ten per cent every two years. These reservations ended in 1960. Anglo-Indians can, of course, still be appointed on merit." },
  "337": { "s": "Temporary education grants for Anglo-Indian institutions, now expired.", "e": "For ten years after 1950, the Union and States continued, on a reducing scale, the special educational grants Anglo-Indian institutions had received before. To get the grant, an institution had to open at least forty per cent of its admissions to other communities. The special concession ended in 1960." },
  "338": { "s": "Creates the National Commission for Scheduled Castes to protect SC rights.", "e": "This article sets up the National Commission for Scheduled Castes, with a Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson and three Members appointed by the President. Its job is to monitor constitutional and legal safeguards for SCs, look into specific complaints of denial of rights, advise on their development, and report to the President every year. Its reports must be placed before Parliament (and, where relevant, State Legislatures) with an explanation of what action was taken and why any recommendation was rejected. While investigating, it has the powers of a civil court, such as summoning people and requiring documents. The Union and State governments must consult it on major policy matters affecting SCs. The original Special Officer system was replaced by a multi-member commission by the 65th Amendment (1990); the 89th Amendment (2003) created a separate commission for STs (Article 338A), and the 102nd Amendment (2018) moved backward classes to a separate commission (Article 338B). The Commission's mandate also covers the Anglo-Indian community." },
  "338A": { "s": "Creates the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes.", "e": "Added by the 89th Amendment (2003), this article sets up a separate National Commission for Scheduled Tribes, with a Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson and three Members appointed by the President. It monitors safeguards for STs, inquires into complaints, advises on their development and reports to the President, with reports placed before Parliament. It has civil-court powers for investigation, and governments must consult it on major policy matters affecting Scheduled Tribes." },
  "338B": { "s": "Gives constitutional status to the National Commission for Backward Classes.", "e": "Added by the 102nd Amendment (2018), this article turned the National Commission for Backward Classes, earlier a statutory body, into a constitutional one. It monitors safeguards for socially and educationally backward classes, inquires into complaints, advises on their development and reports to the President, and it has civil-court powers while investigating. The Union and States must consult it on major policy matters affecting backward classes. A proviso added by the 105th Amendment (2021) says States need not consult it when preparing their own backward class lists under Article 342A(3)." },
  "339": { "s": "Union oversight of Scheduled Areas and tribal welfare in the States.", "e": "The President may at any time, and had to after ten years, appoint a Commission to report on the administration of Scheduled Areas and the welfare of Scheduled Tribes. The Union government can also direct a State to draw up and carry out schemes considered essential for tribal welfare. This gives the Centre a supervisory role over how States treat tribal communities." },
  "340": { "s": "President may appoint a Commission to study backward classes and suggest remedies.", "e": "The President can appoint a Commission to investigate the conditions of socially and educationally backward classes, the difficulties they face, and the steps and grants needed to improve their situation. Its report, along with a note on action taken, must be laid before Parliament. The Kaka Kalelkar Commission (1953) and the Mandal Commission (1979), whose report led to reservations for Other Backward Classes in central jobs, were appointed under this article." },
  "341": { "s": "President notifies which castes are Scheduled Castes; only Parliament can change the list.", "e": "The President, after consulting the Governor in the case of a State, issues a public notification listing the castes, races or tribes (or parts of them) that count as Scheduled Castes in that State or Union territory. Once issued, the list can be changed only by a law of Parliament, not by a fresh notification or by State action. The Supreme Court has held that courts and State governments cannot add to or subtract from this list. This list determines who can benefit from SC reservations and safeguards." },
  "342": { "s": "President notifies which communities are Scheduled Tribes; only Parliament can change the list.", "e": "The President, after consulting the Governor in the case of a State, publishes a notification listing the tribes or tribal communities that are Scheduled Tribes in that State or Union territory. After that, only Parliament can include or exclude a group, by passing a law. The list governs eligibility for ST reservations and protections." },
  "342A": { "s": "Central list of backward classes by President; States may keep their own lists.", "e": "Added by the 102nd Amendment (2018), this article lets the President, after consulting the Governor, notify the socially and educationally backward classes in the 'Central List', which applies for the Central Government's purposes. Only Parliament can then add or remove groups from the Central List. In the Maratha reservation case (Jaishri Laxmanrao Patil, 2021), the Supreme Court read the 102nd Amendment as taking away the States' power to identify backward classes. Parliament responded with the 105th Amendment (2021), which added clause (3) and clarified that every State and Union territory may prepare and maintain its own list for its own purposes, which can differ from the Central List. As a result, there are now two separate lists: one for central jobs and institutions, and one for each State." },
  "343": { "s": "Hindi in Devanagari is the Union's official language; English continues alongside.", "e": "The official language of the Union is Hindi written in the Devanagari script, with international numerals (1, 2, 3) for official purposes. The Constitution planned for English to be used for fifteen years, until 1965, while Hindi was gradually introduced. It also allowed Parliament to continue English after that period by law. Following strong objections from non-Hindi-speaking States, Parliament passed the Official Languages Act, 1963, under which English continues to be used alongside Hindi for official Union purposes without a fixed end date. Note that the Constitution does not declare any 'national language'; it only speaks of an official language." },
  "344": { "s": "Commission and parliamentary committee to review progress of Hindi as official language.", "e": "The President had to set up an Official Language Commission after five and again after ten years from 1950 to recommend steps for increasing the use of Hindi and limiting English in Union work. A Committee of thirty MPs (twenty from the Lok Sabha, ten from the Rajya Sabha) then examines the Commission's recommendations and reports to the President. The Commission must keep in mind the interests of people from non-Hindi-speaking areas, especially in public services. The first Commission, chaired by B.G. Kher, was appointed in 1955." },
  "345": { "s": "Each State may choose its own official language or languages.", "e": "A State Legislature may adopt by law one or more languages used in the State, or Hindi, as the language for its official work. Until it does so, English continues to be used. This is why States conduct their affairs in languages such as Tamil, Bengali, Marathi or Hindi." },
  "346": { "s": "Language for communication between States and with the Union.", "e": "The language officially used by the Union is also the language for communication between States and between a State and the Union. However, two or more States may agree to use Hindi between themselves. Given the Official Languages Act, English continues to be widely used for such communication." },
  "347": { "s": "President may require a State to recognise a language spoken by a significant group.", "e": "If a substantial part of a State's population wants a language they speak to be officially recognised, and a demand is made, the President may direct the State to recognise it throughout the State or in part of it, for purposes he specifies. This protects linguistic minorities within a State." },
  "348": { "s": "English is the language of the Supreme Court, High Courts and official texts of laws.", "e": "Until Parliament provides otherwise, proceedings in the Supreme Court and High Courts, and the authoritative texts of Bills, Acts, Ordinances, rules and orders, are in English. A Governor, with the President's consent, may allow Hindi or the State's official language in High Court proceedings, but judgments, decrees and orders still have to be in English. Where a State uses another language for its laws, an official English translation is treated as the authoritative English text." },
  "349": { "s": "Special procedure for language laws during the first fifteen years.", "e": "For fifteen years after 1950, no Bill changing the language of courts or laws under Article 348 could be introduced in Parliament without the President's prior approval. The President could give approval only after considering the Official Language Commission's recommendations and the parliamentary Committee's report. This safeguard ended in 1965." },
  "350": { "s": "Anyone may petition the government in any language used in the Union or State.", "e": "Every person may submit a complaint or request for redress of a grievance to any Union or State officer or authority in any language used in the Union or that State. Officials cannot refuse to receive a petition only because of its language." },
  "350A": { "s": "States should provide primary education in the mother tongue for linguistic minorities.", "e": "Added by the 7th Amendment (1956), this article asks every State and local authority to try to provide adequate facilities for teaching children of linguistic minority groups in their mother tongue at the primary stage. The President may issue directions to States to secure this." },
  "350B": { "s": "A Special Officer monitors safeguards for linguistic minorities.", "e": "Added by the 7th Amendment (1956), this article provides for a Special Officer for linguistic minorities, appointed by the President. The officer investigates how constitutional safeguards for linguistic minorities are working and reports to the President, and the reports are placed before Parliament and sent to the State governments concerned. The office is known as the Commissioner for Linguistic Minorities." },
  "351": { "s": "The Union must promote and develop the Hindi language.", "e": "The Union has a duty to spread and develop Hindi so that it can express all elements of India's composite culture. In doing so, it should enrich Hindi by drawing on Hindustani, the other languages of the Eighth Schedule, and mainly Sanskrit for vocabulary, without changing Hindi's essential character. This is a guiding duty and does not make Hindi compulsory for citizens." },
  "352": { "s": "President may declare a national emergency for war, external aggression or armed rebellion.", "e": "If the President is satisfied that India's security, or that of any part, is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion, he may proclaim an emergency for the whole country or a part of it, even before the actual threat materialises if danger is imminent. Many safeguards were added by the 44th Amendment (1978) after the experience of the 1975-77 Emergency: 'internal disturbance' was replaced by the narrower 'armed rebellion'; the President can act only on the written decision of the Union Cabinet; and the proclamation must be approved by both Houses of Parliament within one month by a special majority (a majority of total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting). Once approved, it lasts six months at a time and needs fresh approval to continue. The Lok Sabha can end it by passing a simple-majority resolution of disapproval, and one-tenth of its members can demand a special sitting for that purpose within fourteen days. The 44th Amendment also removed the clause, added by the 38th Amendment (1975), that had made the President's satisfaction immune from court review. National emergencies have been declared three times: in 1962 (China war), 1971 (Pakistan war) and 1975 (internal disturbance)." },
  "353": { "s": "During national emergency, the Centre can direct States and legislate on any subject.", "e": "While a national emergency is in force, the Union government can give directions to any State on how to exercise its executive power, and Parliament can make laws giving powers and duties to Union authorities even on subjects outside the Union List. The federal system thus becomes effectively unitary during the emergency. If the emergency covers only part of India, these powers can extend to other States too, insofar as the threat arises from the affected area." },
  "354": { "s": "President may modify sharing of revenues between Centre and States during emergency.", "e": "During a national emergency, the President may order changes to the constitutional scheme for distributing tax revenues between the Union and States (Articles 268 to 279). Such changes cannot last beyond the end of the financial year in which the emergency ends, and every such order must be placed before Parliament." },
  "355": { "s": "The Union must protect States and ensure they are governed constitutionally.", "e": "The Union has a duty to protect every State against external aggression and internal disturbance, and to make sure each State government is run according to the Constitution. This provides the basis for the Centre deploying armed forces to help States and for its powers under Articles 356 and 365. This duty does not by itself justify removing a State government; any such action must still meet the conditions of Article 356, as the Supreme Court stressed in S.R. Bommai (1994)." },
  "356": { "s": "President's Rule: Centre may take over a State whose constitutional machinery fails.", "e": "If the President, on a report from the Governor or otherwise, is satisfied that a State cannot be governed according to the Constitution, he may proclaim 'President's Rule'. The Union then takes over the State government's functions, and Parliament exercises the State Legislature's law-making powers, though the High Court's powers cannot be touched. The proclamation must be approved by both Houses of Parliament within two months and then lasts six months at a time, up to a maximum of three years. Under clause (5), in the form substituted by the 44th Amendment (1978), continuing it beyond one year needs a national emergency to be in force and a certificate from the Election Commission that Assembly elections cannot be held. Because this power was frequently misused against opposition-ruled States, the Supreme Court in S.R. Bommai (1994) laid down important limits: the proclamation is open to judicial review; the question of whether a government has lost its majority should normally be tested on the floor of the Assembly; and the Assembly may only be suspended, not dissolved, until Parliament approves the proclamation. The Court can restore a dismissed government if the proclamation is found invalid. The Court also held that secularism is part of the basic structure and that a State government acting against it can be a ground for this action." },
  "357": { "s": "How State laws are made during President's Rule.", "e": "When Parliament takes over a State Legislature's powers under Article 356, it can delegate law-making for that State to the President, who may further delegate it. The President can also authorise spending from the State's Consolidated Fund while the Lok Sabha is not in session, subject to later approval by Parliament. Laws made during President's Rule remain in force after it ends, until the State Legislature changes or repeals them." },
  "358": { "s": "Article 19 freedoms are suspended during an emergency due to war or external aggression.", "e": "When a national emergency is declared on the ground of war or external aggression (but not armed rebellion), the six freedoms in Article 19, such as speech, assembly and movement, no longer limit the State's power to make laws or take executive action. Laws made in this way cease to operate, to the extent they would otherwise be invalid, once the emergency ends, though actions taken in the meantime stay valid. The 44th Amendment (1978) narrowed this power: it now applies only to war or external aggression, and only to laws that expressly state they relate to the emergency, and to executive action under such laws. If the emergency covers only part of India, such laws can still apply elsewhere if the threat originates from the emergency area." },
  "359": { "s": "President may suspend court enforcement of fundamental rights, except Articles 20 and 21.", "e": "During a national emergency, the President may by order suspend the right to approach courts to enforce specified fundamental rights, for the duration of the emergency or a shorter period. While such an order is in force, laws and executive actions cannot be challenged for violating those rights, but laws must state that they relate to the emergency. The rights themselves are not abolished, only their enforcement through courts is suspended. Crucially, after the 44th Amendment (1978), the President cannot suspend the enforcement of Article 20 (protection in respect of conviction for offences) or Article 21 (right to life and personal liberty). This change was a response to the Emergency of 1975-77 and the Supreme Court's decision in ADM Jabalpur (1976), which had held that no one could go to court to challenge illegal detention during the emergency; that decision was later expressly overruled in K.S. Puttaswamy (2017). Every order must be laid before Parliament." },
  "359A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly applied emergency provisions specially to Punjab.", "e": "Added by the 59th Amendment (1988) to allow special emergency measures in Punjab during militancy, including on the ground of internal disturbance. It was omitted by the 63rd Amendment (1989)." },
  "360": { "s": "President may declare a financial emergency if India's financial stability is threatened.", "e": "If the President is satisfied that India's financial stability or credit, or that of any part of it, is threatened, he may declare a financial emergency. The proclamation must be approved by both Houses of Parliament within two months; once approved, it continues until revoked, without a fixed time limit. During it, the Union can direct States to follow rules of financial propriety, require reduction of salaries and allowances of State employees, and require States to reserve Money Bills for the President's consideration. The President may also order cuts in the salaries of Union employees, including Supreme Court and High Court judges. The 44th Amendment (1978) rewrote the parliamentary-approval clause and removed a clause (inserted by the 38th Amendment) that had sought to bar courts from reviewing the declaration. A financial emergency has never been declared in India." },
  "361": { "s": "President and Governors are immune from court proceedings while in office.", "e": "The President and the Governors cannot be made answerable in any court for anything done in the exercise of their official powers and duties. During their term, no criminal case can be started or continued against them, and no court can order their arrest or imprisonment. Civil cases relating to their personal acts can be filed only after two months' written notice. This immunity is personal: people can still bring cases against the Government of India or the State government for those actions, and the President's conduct can be examined in impeachment proceedings under Article 61. Courts have held that this personal immunity does not prevent them from examining whether official actions, such as a Governor's report under Article 356, were lawful or mala fide." },
  "361A": { "s": "Media protected when publishing true reports of legislative proceedings.", "e": "No one can be sued or prosecuted for publishing in a newspaper, or broadcasting, a substantially true report of proceedings of Parliament or a State Legislature, unless it is proved that the publication was malicious. The protection does not cover reports of secret sittings. It was added by the 44th Amendment (1978)." },
  "361B": { "s": "Defectors disqualified under anti-defection law cannot hold paid political posts.", "e": "Added by the 91st Amendment (2003), this article says that a legislator disqualified for defection under the Tenth Schedule also cannot hold any 'remunerative political post' (a paid office under the government or a government-owned body) until the end of their original term, or until they win an election again, whichever is earlier. This is meant to remove the incentive of being rewarded with office for switching sides." },
  "362": { "s": "Omitted — formerly protected the rights and privileges of former princely rulers.", "e": "This required governments to respect the personal rights, privileges and dignities of the rulers of former princely States under their merger agreements. It was omitted by the 26th Amendment (1971), which ended the privy purses and recognition of rulers." },
  "363": { "s": "Courts cannot hear disputes arising from pre-1950 treaties with princely rulers.", "e": "No court, including the Supreme Court, can decide disputes arising out of treaties, agreements, covenants or similar instruments signed before 1950 between rulers of princely States and the Government of India or its predecessors. The President may, however, seek the Supreme Court's advisory opinion on such matters under Article 143. This kept the politically sensitive integration agreements out of litigation." },
  "363A": { "s": "Abolishes recognition of princely rulers and their privy purses.", "e": "Added by the 26th Amendment (1971), this article ended official recognition of former rulers of princely States and their successors, and abolished the privy purses (annual payments) promised to them at the time of integration. All rights and obligations relating to privy purses were extinguished. It followed the Supreme Court's decision in the Madhav Rao Scindia case (1970), which had struck down the President's earlier order derecognising the rulers." },
  "364": { "s": "President may modify how laws apply to major ports and aerodromes.", "e": "The President may, by public notification, direct that a law of Parliament or a State, or an existing law, shall not apply, or shall apply with changes, to a major port or aerodrome. This allows special legal arrangements for these strategically and economically important places." },
  "365": { "s": "A State's failure to follow Union directions can justify President's Rule.", "e": "If a State does not comply with lawful directions given by the Union in exercise of its executive power, the President may treat this as a situation in which the State's government cannot be carried on under the Constitution. This opens the way to action under Article 356. The Sarkaria Commission recommended that this be a last resort, used only after the State has been warned and given a chance to comply." },
  "366": { "s": "Dictionary of key terms used in the Constitution.", "e": "This article defines many expressions used throughout the Constitution, unless the context requires otherwise. Examples include 'agricultural income', 'Anglo-Indian', 'existing law', 'High Court', 'Scheduled Castes' and 'Scheduled Tribes' (as notified under Articles 341 and 342), and 'socially and educationally backward classes' (as deemed under Article 342A). The 101st Amendment (2016) added definitions of 'goods and services tax' and 'services' for GST. These definitions help ensure that the terms are read consistently across the document." },
  "367": { "s": "Rules for interpreting the Constitution, including use of the General Clauses Act.", "e": "The General Clauses Act, 1897, applies to the interpretation of the Constitution as it does to an ordinary Act, unless the context requires otherwise. References to laws of Parliament or a State Legislature include Ordinances issued by the President or a Governor. A 'foreign State' means any State other than India, though the President may declare a particular State not to be foreign for certain purposes. In 2019, a Presidential order applying to Jammu and Kashmir added an interpretation clause here that was used to change how Article 370(3) was read; the Supreme Court in December 2023 held that use of this article to effectively amend Article 370 was impermissible, while upholding the abrogation on other grounds." },
  "368": { "s": "Parliament's power and procedure to amend the Constitution, limited by the basic structure.", "e": "Parliament can amend the Constitution by adding, changing or repealing provisions. An amendment Bill can be introduced in either House and must be passed in each House by a majority of its total membership and two-thirds of members present and voting; the President must then give assent. Amendments affecting federal features, such as the election of the President, the powers of the Union and States, the Supreme Court and High Courts, the Seventh Schedule lists, the representation of States in Parliament, GST Council (Article 279A) or this article itself, also need ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures. Some provisions can be changed by ordinary law, and they are not covered by this article. The scope of the amending power has been central to Indian constitutional history: after Golak Nath (1967) held that fundamental rights could not be amended, the 24th Amendment (1971) reaffirmed Parliament's power. In Kesavananda Bharati (1973), the Supreme Court held that Parliament may amend any part of the Constitution but cannot destroy its 'basic structure', such as democracy, judicial review, federalism and secularism. Clauses (4) and (5), added by the 42nd Amendment (1976) to bar judicial review of amendments, still appear in the text but were struck down in Minerva Mills (1980)." },
  "369": { "s": "Temporary five-year power for Parliament over certain State subjects.", "e": "For the first five years after 1950, Parliament could make laws on trade, production and distribution of essential goods like textiles, foodstuffs, coal, iron, steel and paper, as if these were in the Concurrent List. Such laws lapsed at the end of that period to the extent Parliament would otherwise not have been competent. The provision is now spent." },
  "370": { "s": "Former special status of Jammu and Kashmir; rendered inoperative in 2019.", "e": "This article gave Jammu and Kashmir a special position: Parliament's law-making power over the State was limited to subjects related to the Instrument of Accession (defence, foreign affairs and communications) and other matters agreed to by the State government, and other parts of the Constitution applied only as the President ordered. Clause (3) allowed the President to declare that the article would cease to operate, on the recommendation of the State's Constituent Assembly. Over the decades, many Presidential orders extended most of the Constitution to the State. In August 2019, during President's Rule in the State, Presidential orders (C.O. 272 and C.O. 273) applied the entire Constitution to Jammu and Kashmir and declared all clauses of Article 370, except a part of clause (1), inoperative; the Jammu and Kashmir Reorganisation Act, 2019 then split the State into two Union territories. The text of Article 370 still appears in the Constitution (including wording altered by those orders, such as 'Legislative Assembly' in clause (3)), but it no longer has practical effect. In December 2023 the Supreme Court upheld the abrogation, holding that Article 370 was a temporary provision and the President could exercise the clause (3) power without the Constituent Assembly's recommendation, though it held that amending Article 370 through Article 367 (as C.O. 272 did) was impermissible. The Court directed that statehood be restored as soon as possible and that Assembly elections be held by 30 September 2024. Elections were held in September–October 2024, but as of November 2026 statehood has not been restored." },
  "371": { "s": "Special development responsibilities of the Governor for regions of Maharashtra and Gujarat.", "e": "The President may give the Governors of Maharashtra and Gujarat special responsibility for setting up separate development boards for regions such as Vidarbha, Marathwada and the rest of Maharashtra, and Saurashtra, Kutch and the rest of Gujarat. The Governor can also ensure fair allocation of development funds across these regions and fair opportunities in technical education, vocational training and State jobs, subject to the needs of the State as a whole. Each board's annual report must be placed before the State Assembly. As rewritten in 1956, Article 371 also had a clause (1) with special provisions for Andhra Pradesh and Punjab, which was removed by the 32nd Amendment (1973). Articles 371A to 371J build on the same idea, providing tailored arrangements for other States, often to protect regional, tribal or cultural interests. These provisions show that Indian federalism allows asymmetric arrangements for particular States." },
  "371A": { "s": "Special protections for Nagaland's customs, customary law and land.", "e": "Added by the 13th Amendment (1962) when Nagaland became a State, this article says no Act of Parliament on Naga religious or social practices, Naga customary law and procedure, civil and criminal justice under customary law, or ownership and transfer of land and its resources applies to Nagaland unless its Legislative Assembly so resolves. The Governor was given special responsibility for law and order while internal disturbances continued, acting in his individual judgment after consulting ministers. It also made special arrangements for the Tuensang district, including a regional council." },
  "371B": { "s": "Special Assembly committee for tribal areas of Assam.", "e": "Added by the 22nd Amendment (1969), this lets the President provide for a committee of the Assam Legislative Assembly made up of members elected from the State's tribal areas (listed in the Sixth Schedule) and other members. The aim is to give tribal-area representatives a dedicated role in matters affecting those areas." },
  "371C": { "s": "Special Assembly committee and Governor's role for the Hill Areas of Manipur.", "e": "Added by the 27th Amendment (1971), this lets the President provide for a committee of the Manipur Assembly made up of members elected from the Hill Areas, and give the Governor special responsibility to make it work. The Governor reports to the President on the administration of the Hill Areas every year, and the Union can give directions to the State about these areas." },
  "371D": { "s": "Equal opportunities in jobs and education across regions of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana.", "e": "Added by the 32nd Amendment (1973) after regional agitations, and later extended to Telangana when it was formed in 2014, this article lets the President make orders ensuring fair opportunities in public employment and education for people from different parts of each State. Such orders can create local cadres, define local areas, and give preference to local candidates in recruitment and admissions. It also allows an Administrative Tribunal to handle service disputes, outside the supervision of the High Court. These provisions override other parts of the Constitution and other laws." },
  "371E": { "s": "Parliament may establish a central university in Andhra Pradesh.", "e": "Added by the 32nd Amendment (1973), this allows Parliament to set up by law a University in Andhra Pradesh. The University of Hyderabad was established under this provision." },
  "371F": { "s": "Special provisions for Sikkim on joining India in 1975.", "e": "Added by the 36th Amendment (1975) when Sikkim became a State, this article deemed Sikkim's existing Assembly to be the State Legislative Assembly and fixed its minimum size at thirty members. It gave Sikkim one seat in the Lok Sabha and allowed Parliament to reserve Assembly seats to protect the rights of different sections of the population. The Governor has special responsibility for peace and for the social and economic advancement of different sections. Existing laws of Sikkim continued until changed by a competent authority." },
  "371G": { "s": "Special protections for Mizoram's customs, customary law and land.", "e": "Added by the 53rd Amendment (1986) when Mizoram became a State, this says no Act of Parliament on Mizo religious or social practices, Mizo customary law and procedure, justice under customary law, or ownership and transfer of land applies to Mizoram unless its Legislative Assembly so resolves. Central laws already in force before statehood continue. The Assembly must have at least forty members." },
  "371H": { "s": "Governor's special law-and-order role in Arunachal Pradesh.", "e": "Added by the 55th Amendment (1986), this gives the Governor of Arunachal Pradesh special responsibility for law and order, to be exercised in his individual judgment after consulting the Council of Ministers. The President may end this special responsibility when it is no longer needed. The State Assembly must have at least thirty members." },
  "371I": { "s": "Goa's Legislative Assembly must have at least thirty members.", "e": "Added by the 56th Amendment (1987) when Goa became a State, this simply sets the minimum size of the Goa Legislative Assembly at thirty members, even though the general rule in Article 170 requires sixty." },
  "371J": { "s": "Special development provisions for the Hyderabad-Karnataka region of Karnataka.", "e": "Added by the 98th Amendment (2012), this lets the President give the Governor of Karnataka special responsibility for a development board for the Hyderabad-Karnataka region (now called Kalyana-Karnataka), fair allocation of development funds, and fair opportunities in jobs and education there. Orders may reserve a share of seats in educational institutions and of State government posts in the region for local people by birth or domicile." },
  "372": { "s": "Laws in force before 1950 continue until changed.", "e": "All laws in force in India just before the Constitution began continue to apply until a competent legislature or authority changes or repeals them, provided they are consistent with the Constitution. The President was given power to adapt these laws to fit the new Constitution for a limited period. This prevented a legal vacuum at the start of the Republic; many colonial-era laws continued under this provision." },
  "372A": { "s": "President's power to adapt laws after the 1956 reorganisation of States.", "e": "Added by the 7th Amendment (1956), this allowed the President, until 1 November 1957, to adapt or modify existing laws to fit the reorganised States and the amended Constitution. Such adaptations could not be challenged in court, but legislatures could later change them." },
  "373": { "s": "Temporary presidential power over preventive detention in 1950.", "e": "Until Parliament made a law under Article 22(7), or for one year from 1950, whichever came first, the President could exercise Parliament's role in Article 22 on preventive detention. Parliament soon passed the Preventive Detention Act, 1950, so this provision is now spent." },
  "374": { "s": "Federal Court judges and pending cases transferred to the new Supreme Court.", "e": "Judges of the Federal Court became the first Judges of the Supreme Court on 26 January 1950, and all cases pending before the Federal Court moved to the Supreme Court. Earlier Federal Court judgments were given the same force as Supreme Court judgments, and pending appeals before the Privy Council were also dealt with. This ensured continuity in the judicial system." },
  "375": { "s": "Existing courts and officials continued to function under the Constitution.", "e": "All courts and all judicial, executive and administrative officers in India continued to perform their functions after the Constitution came into force, subject to its provisions. This ensured that the machinery of government kept running without interruption." },
  "376": { "s": "Existing High Court judges continued under the new Constitution.", "e": "Judges of High Courts in the former Provinces became Judges of the corresponding State High Courts in 1950, and judges in princely States' High Courts continued for a period set by the President. Such judges could become Chief Justices even if not Indian citizens. This was a transitional provision." },
  "377": { "s": "The Auditor-General of India became the Comptroller and Auditor-General.", "e": "The person serving as Auditor-General of India just before the Constitution began became the first Comptroller and Auditor-General of India under the Constitution, with the salary and rights provided for that office. It is a transitional provision." },
  "378": { "s": "Existing Public Service Commission members continued in office.", "e": "Members of the Federal Public Service Commission and provincial Public Service Commissions became members of the Union and State Public Service Commissions in 1950, completing their existing terms. It ensured continuity in recruitment bodies." },
  "378A": { "s": "Fixed the term of the first Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly after 1956.", "e": "Added by the 7th Amendment (1956), this provided that the Andhra Pradesh Legislative Assembly constituted under the States Reorganisation Act, 1956 would last five years from the date fixed in that Act, after which it would stand dissolved. It is a spent transitional provision." },
  "379": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about the provisional Parliament and its presiding officers.", "e": "This dealt with the Constituent Assembly acting as the provisional Parliament until the first elections. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956) as it had served its purpose." },
  "380": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about the provisional President.", "e": "This provided for the President until the first President was elected under the Constitution. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "381": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about the President's interim Council of Ministers.", "e": "This dealt with the Council of Ministers advising the President during the transition. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "382": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about provisional Legislatures for Part A States.", "e": "This provided for interim Legislatures in the former Part A States until elections were held. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "383": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about the Governors of Provinces during transition.", "e": "This continued the Governors of Provinces as Governors of the corresponding States until new appointments. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "384": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about Governors' interim Councils of Ministers.", "e": "This dealt with Councils of Ministers advising Governors during the transition. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "385": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about provisional Legislatures for Part B States.", "e": "This provided for interim Legislatures in the former Part B (princely) States. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "386": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about Councils of Ministers for Part B States.", "e": "This dealt with Councils of Ministers in the former Part B States during the transition. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "387": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about determining population for early elections.", "e": "This allowed special rules for determining population for the first elections, before the first census under the Constitution. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "388": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about filling casual vacancies in provisional legislatures.", "e": "This dealt with filling vacancies in the provisional Parliament and State Legislatures. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "389": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about Bills pending in pre-1950 legislatures.", "e": "This allowed Bills pending in the Dominion and Provincial Legislatures to continue in the new Legislatures. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "390": { "s": "Omitted — formerly about finances between January and March 1950.", "e": "This regularised money received or spent between the Constitution's commencement and 31 March 1950. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "391": { "s": "Omitted — formerly let the President amend the First and Fourth Schedules.", "e": "This allowed the President to amend the First and Fourth Schedules in certain contingencies during the transition. It was omitted by the 7th Amendment (1956)." },
  "392": { "s": "President's temporary power to remove difficulties in the transition to the Constitution.", "e": "The President could, by order, adapt the Constitution's provisions for a period to remove difficulties in moving from the Government of India Act, 1935 to the new Constitution. This power could not be used after the first meeting of the newly elected Parliament in 1952, and every order had to be laid before Parliament. Before 26 January 1950, the Governor-General exercised this power." },
  "393": { "s": "The Constitution is called the Constitution of India.", "e": "This article gives the document its short title: the Constitution of India." },
  "394": { "s": "Some articles began on 26 November 1949; the rest on 26 January 1950.", "e": "This article, along with provisions on citizenship, elections, definitions, interpretation and certain transitional matters, came into force immediately on adoption on 26 November 1949. The rest of the Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950, which the Constitution calls its 'commencement' and which is celebrated as Republic Day." },
  "394A": { "s": "Provides for an authoritative Hindi text of the Constitution.", "e": "Added by the 58th Amendment (1987), this requires the President to publish an authoritative Hindi translation of the Constitution and of every amendment. The Hindi text is to be read as having the same meaning as the English original, and if difficulties arise, the President must revise it suitably." },
  "395": { "s": "Repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935.", "e": "This article repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935, along with laws amending or supplementing the latter, but keeps the Abolition of Privy Council Jurisdiction Act, 1949. By cutting the legal link with British parliamentary statutes, it marks India's constitutional break from colonial rule and its status as a sovereign republic." }
});
